  <div class="nbd-section">
    <h3>Safe Mode / offline (this Unraid)</h3>
    <p class="nbd-muted">
      In <strong>Safe Mode</strong> the plugin UI does not load. Use the console or SSH.
      Plugin-managed hosts store state under <code>/var/run/nbdexport/</code>; pull job logs under <code>/var/log/nbdexport/</code>.
    </p>
    <pre class="nbd-cli"># Stop every plugin-managed Host export
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/NBDExport/scripts/nbd-export-stop all

# Stop one export by id (see Status tab, or JSON under /var/run/nbdexport/)
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/NBDExport/scripts/nbd-export-stop &lt;export-id&gt;

# List qemu-nbd processes (plugin and any you started by hand)
ps aux | grep '[q]emu-nbd'

# Kill a hand-started qemu-nbd (example — use the real PID)
kill &lt;pid&gt;</pre>
    <blockquote class="inline_help">
      <strong>Enable NBD Export = No</strong> under Settings also stops plugin-managed hosts after Apply.
      It does not kill a <code>qemu-nbd</code> you started manually on the console.
    </blockquote>
  </div>

  <div class="nbd-section">
    <h3>CLI (same path the UI wraps)</h3>
    <pre class="nbd-cli"># Host read-only on a private bind IP (change --port per disk)
qemu-nbd --read-only --persistent --shared=2 \
  --bind=&lt;BIND_IP&gt; --port=10809 --format=raw &lt;DEVICE&gt;

# On the Unraid that will save the image
qemu-img info nbd://&lt;BIND_IP&gt;:10809
qemu-img convert -p -f raw -O qcow2 -t writeback -W \
  nbd://&lt;BIND_IP&gt;:10809 /mnt/cache/domains/example.qcow2</pre>
    <p class="nbd-muted" style="margin:0.4em 0 0">
      Prefer a real path under <code>/mnt/cache</code>, <code>/mnt/diskN</code>, or a pool — not only <code>/mnt/user</code> — for large pulls.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="nbd-section">
    <h3>Host a disk on another Linux (generic)</h3>
    <ol style="line-height:1.5;margin:0.4em 0 0.6em 1.25em;padding:0">
      <li>Install <code>qemu-nbd</code> (package name varies by distro; often part of <code>qemu-img</code> / <code>qemu-tools</code>).</li>
      <li>Prefer <strong>read-only</strong>. Bind a <strong>private</strong> address Unraid can reach — not an open WAN listener.</li>
      <li>Start the export (example):
        <pre class="nbd-cli" style="margin:0.4em 0">qemu-nbd --read-only --persistent --shared=4 \
  --bind=&lt;BIND_IP&gt; --port=10809 --format=raw &lt;DEVICE&gt;</pre>
      </li>
      <li>Open the firewall <strong>only</strong> for the Unraid (or private subnet) that will Pull — then remove the allow when finished.
        <pre class="nbd-cli" style="margin:0.4em 0"># firewalld example (adjust zone/subnet/port)
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="&lt;CLIENT_SUBNET_OR_IP&gt;" port port="10809" protocol="tcp" accept'
# When done:
firewall-cmd --remove-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="&lt;CLIENT_SUBNET_OR_IP&gt;" port port="10809" protocol="tcp" accept'</pre>
      </li>
      <li>On Unraid: <strong>Pull</strong> tab → <code>nbd://&lt;BIND_IP&gt;:10809</code> → file under <code>/mnt/…</code>.</li>
      <li>Stop <code>qemu-nbd</code> on the peer and close the temporary firewall hole.</li>
    </ol>
    <blockquote class="inline_help">
      NBD has no authentication. Anyone who can reach the port can read (or write, if you hosted writable) raw sectors.
      Keep the allow-list narrow. Full guide:
      <a href="https://github.com/ibigsnet/NBDExport/blob/main/docs/peer-host-linux.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">peer-host-linux.md ↗</a>
    </blockquote>
  </div>

